manding new-knowledge storage (Tables 3,4) and in-context learning (Figures 3,4), our model provides a significant performance advantage even under matched FLOPs/parameter budgets (Appendix E, Figure 12). The O(n) cost for very long contexts is a known challenge; we explicitly mention efficient hashing and hierarchical memory as future directions (Section 8) and can expand this discussion.\n\n**Evaluation Rigor:** Beyond the data/baseline clarifications above, our evaluation is extensive across three dimensions using established benchmarks (RULER, MMLU, etc.). The finding that transformers trained on 8x more data still lag (Section 6) is a robust, data-driven argument for architectural innovation. We believe the added transparency will solidify the evaluation's credibility.\n\nWe are confident these revisions will address the reviewer's concerns, enhancing the paper's clarity, reproducibility, and impact, and we hope this leads to a positive reassessment."
